# 🪟 Windows Theory

# Windows Services

A Windows Service is a background program that runs automatically without user interaction.

Purpose:

- Supports operating system functionality
- Runs continuously in the background
- Starts automatically or manually depending on configuration

Examples:

- Print Spooler
- DHCP Client
- DNS Client
- Windows Update
- Windows Defender

---

# Opening Windows Services

Method 1:

```
Windows + R
```

Type:

```
services.msc
```

Method 2:

Search:

```
Services
```

from the Start Menu.

---

# Service Status

A Windows Service can be:

- Running
- Stopped
- Disabled

---

# Print Spooler Service

Purpose:

The Print Spooler service manages print jobs sent from Windows to the printer.

Responsibilities:

- Receives print requests
- Places documents in the print queue
- Sends print jobs to the printer

If Print Spooler stops:

- Print jobs remain in the queue
- Users cannot print

---

# DHCP Client Service

Purpose:

Automatically obtains network configuration from a DHCP server.

The DHCP Client receives:

- IP Address
- Subnet Mask
- Default Gateway
- DNS Server

Without DHCP Client:

- Computer may not receive an IP address automatically
- Network connectivity problems may occur

---

# DNS Client Service

Purpose:

Resolves domain names into IP addresses and caches DNS information.

Example:

```
www.google.com
```

↓

```
142.250.xxx.xxx
```

Benefits:

- Faster website access
- Reduced DNS lookup time
- Cached DNS records

